# ThreadSanitizer suppressions.
#
# Every entry here must name a report that TSan CANNOT reason about correctly,
# not one we would rather not fix. Anything with a qtPilot frame in its stack is
# a real finding and does not belong in this file.

# Qt is linked as a release, non-instrumented shared library. TSan cannot see
# happens-before edges established inside uninstrumented code, so any
# synchronization Qt performs internally is invisible to it: a QObject allocated
# on the main thread and released on a QThread Qt itself started is reported as a
# race even though Qt ordered the two.
#
# Observed as: "data race ... in operator delete", with both stacks entirely
# inside libQt6Core (every Qt frame unsymbolized) and no qtPilot symbol in
# either. Reproduced in test_qml_interaction and test_qml_delegate_ids on
# Linux/GCC; not reported by clang's TSan on macOS.
#
# This is the same class as the QRecursiveMutex finding in docs/SANITIZERS.md —
# TSan blind to a synchronization primitive it cannot instrument. That one was
# fixable because the primitive was ours; this one is inside Qt.
#
# TRADE-OFF, stated plainly: this is broad. It will also mask a genuine qtPilot
# race whose stack has a Qt Core frame in it. The narrower alternative is to
# build Qt with TSan, which is not practical in CI. If a race is suspected in
# code reached through Qt, re-run with this file removed.
#
# `race:` matches a suppression string against a frame's function, file OR module
# name. Qt's frames here are unsymbolized, so the module name is what matches.
# `called_from_lib` was tried first and does NOT match: it suppresses code called
# FROM the named library, whereas these tests call INTO Qt.
race:libQt6Core
